 Axios Future of Defense 
 
 
 
 
 By Thomas Novelly &middot; Nov 12, 2026 
 
 
 Good morning, y'all! It's Thursday and we've got a ton of news to unpack.

 🎤 But, first: We're one week out from the Axios Future of Defense Summit! I'm excited to dive into the latest defense and space trends with some big names .

 🌎 Watch this space: Let's see what's at stake in a congressional Elon Musk probe, reveal some exclusive space industry news and glance at Anduril's satellite tech.

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: SpaceX scrutiny 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photos: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The new Democratic congressional majority is vowing a slew of investigations into Elon Musk's business empire — potentially putting the military's vast portfolio of contracts with SpaceX under the microscope.

 Why it matters: Attempts to hold Musk accountable will run up against a basic reality that for critical military and space missions, the government has no replacement for SpaceX — and likely won't for years.

 The big picture: How the military became so reliant on a single company may itself be an area of inquiry, congressional aides and former U.S. officials told me.

 "There's enough questionable appearances here that I think it's in everybody's interest to go find out what really happened," a former Pentagon official told me, referring to Musk's political influence and the federal contracts SpaceX has accrued. Practically, it will be years before SpaceX competitors offer technology that allows the Defense Department to diversify to non-Musk companies. Musk this week almost dared the government to try, writing on X that he was "happy to cancel any that the government doesn't want. Just say the word!" By the numbers: Since Musk left the U.S. government as an adviser in May 2025, SpaceX has netted at least $10 billion in new contracts, according to a new report from the left-leaning nonprofit Democracy Defenders Fund . 

 That includes two major Space Force contracts: One for nearly $2.3 billion to build a sprawling network of communication satellites and another nearly $4.2 billion award to develop a space-based missile tracking radar. In September, NASA awarded $946 million to SpaceX for three missions to transport astronauts to the International Space Station. Nearly 94% of all orbital U.S. launches were from SpaceX last year, per the American Enterprise Institute's data . So far, in 2026, Musk's rockets have accounted for roughly 90% of those missions. Zoom in: The new Congress is likely to investigate Musk's role in throttling Starlink over Ukraine and the circumstances surrounding SpaceX contracts.

 "How many hearings have the Republicans held focusing on SpaceX and its abuses of its contracts or its actions in Ukraine? Zero," one Democratic Senate aide said. The Senate Armed Services Committee, which helps craft defense spending policy, is also likely "to have a different posture towards how [the Defense Department] handles its SpaceX contracts" starting in January, the aide said. What they're saying: Clayton Swope, a senior space fellow at the Center for Strategic and International Studies, said there's been a long-standing fear in the defense community that the military has put "all eggs in the SpaceX basket."

 "That's always been kind of an underlying current, even within the Pentagon, that we're very uncomfortable with reliance on one vendor," Swope said. "But, then, here we are." State of play: Despite those concerns, SpaceX's satellite and rocket technology has allowed the U.S. to maintain its superiority in space.

 But competitors that lag behind now see an opening for the Pentagon to adopt alternatives. SpaceX's looming retirement of the Falcon 9 rocket, for example, has reportedly left the Space Force in a bind for future launches. This week, the Defense Department's Office of Strategic Capital signed a $150 million conditional loan commitment with SpaceX competitor Rocket Lab "to meet growing commercial and national security demand for access to space." Yes, but: Dave Cavossa, the Commercial Space Federation's president, told me "Starlink and Starshield are one-of-a-kind capabilities" and said that competitors, like Amazon Leo, are a year or two away from becoming operational.

 "SpaceX has built a massive lead today ... The new entrants have to run really hard to catch up," Cavossa said. Reality check: Musk still has the firm backing of Trump and his allies.

 Shortly before the election, the president vowed to " protect " him amid Democratic scrutiny. New White House Chief of Staff Stephen Miller can now weigh in on Musk's government dealings after his wife left her role with SpaceX, The Atlantic reported. "He's untouchable," Fox News host Jesse Watters said of Musk this week . "He's reached a level in this country that, a subpoena? A hearing? It's nothing." The White House and SpaceX did not respond to requests for comment.

 The bottom line: Another former Pentagon official argued that the military's heavy reliance on Musk is precisely why he deserves to be investigated.

 "Strong national security requires actual competition to both keep costs competitive and to prevent amassing of power in any one individual or company," the person said. 
 
