 &nbsp; 
 
 
 3. Exclusive: Trusted Space expansion 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Natalie Peeples/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Trusted Space , a space software company, has purchased Falcon ExoDynamics, a firm that builds technology for maneuverable satellites.

 Why it matters: As the number of satellites surges, there likely won't be enough Space Force Guardians to monitor and operate them all.

 Investors see autonomous software and dynamic satellites as key developments in orbital warfare. Between the lines: The purchase, guided by Washington Harbour Partners, puts autonomy-focused hardware and software together.

 What he's saying: Trusted Space CEO Robert MacMillan told me "there are a small number of Guardians that are flying an ever-increasing number of satellites." The cognitive load will eventually become a burden, he said. 

 "Have we reached that tipping point yet? I don't think so," MacMillan said. "Is it coming, as we go to more and more proliferated constellations? Certainly." Follow the money : Trusted Space and Falcon ExoDynamics have both netted space contracts recently.

 In August, the Air Force Research Laboratory awarded an almost $15 million contract to Virginia-based Trusted Space for autonomous satellite experiments. That same month, California's Falcon ExoDynamics was part of a team that secured a $24 million joint Defense Innovation Unit and Space Development Agency award to pull dead satellites out of orbit. What's next: MacMillan said Trusted Space is focused on building out a new satellite prototype and is working towards orbital experiments.

 "We've got to move quickly, demonstrate our capability, establish that trust and autonomy with our operators, so that we can meet the national security needs for the United States," he said. 
 
